{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The reference ABI contains nine functions: `shift_array`, `process_string`, `apply_bitmask`, `init_matrix`, `compare_allocations`, `arity2`, `arity3`, `arity4`, and `arity`. One important source/header discrepancy is real ABI behavior: `arity` is defined with an `unsigned char` length even though the header declares `int`, so the implementation must preserve low-byte truncation.\n\nI’ll now add a dependency-free `cdylib`. The Rust exports will use C integer/pointer types, call the process allocator for allocation-sensitive behavior, preserve check and call order, and use wrapping arithmetic where the optimized C implementation observes machine-width results. Then I’ll build, diff dynamic exports, and run each function against the C reference over boundary and representative inputs."}}
